// src/kernel/core/net/nic_dispatch.pdx -- R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012)
//
// The single-active-NIC dispatch shim. One `_active_nic_kind` global,
// one probe helper that walks the boot-order candidates and reports
// the first one that answers, and four cmp/je routers -- one for TX,
// one for RX poll, one for RX dispatch (l2_rx handoff), one for MAC
// read -- that fan out over the three NIC kinds KIND_NIC (0x1AD)
// enumerates (E1000E / VIRTIO / RTL8139).
//
// This module owns no ring, drives no controller, and mints no
// capability. The tx / rx_poll / mac routers are not on any ISR path;
// nic_dispatch_rx_dispatch (added by R91.M1-004) IS -- it is what
// e1000e_irq_handler calls per RX descriptor to hand the frame body
// up to l2_rx_handle, and what future virtio-net (R91.M3) / rtl8139
// (R91.M4) IRQ handlers will call for the same purpose. The module
// exists so that l2_tx_send / ipv4_tx_send / e1000e_irq_handler's
// l2_rx_handle callers (R91.M1-003, R91.M1-004) can stop reading
// e1000e's (bar0, tx_ring_pa) globals directly and start asking
// "what kind of NIC is active, and what is its send / poll / dispatch
// / MAC entry point" -- so that R91.M3 (virtio-net) and R91.M4
// (rtl8139) land as new arms in this file rather than as duplicated
// L2/L3 stacks.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  WHY THIS IS A cmp/je CHAIN AND NOT A @jump_table
// =============================================================================
//
// The R91 plan (design/networking/r91-plan.md §4.1) called out
// paideia-as's `@jump_table` attribute as a candidate for the dispatch
// layer, with the load-bearing caveat "before committing to it, verify
// it is actually lowered and not merely forward-declared -- the
// paideia-as CHANGELOG has a documented history of trait declared,
// lowering deferred gaps".
//
// The verification, done at issue landing:
//
//   * PA-R15-009 (paideia-as #964) landed the `@jump_table` PARSER +
//     AST + P0270/P0271 diagnostics. The v0.15 CHANGELOG bullet is
//     explicit: "codegen deferred to #1032."
//   * PA-R15-009b (paideia-as #1032) tracks the codegen synthesis pass
//     -- rodata table + memory-indirect jmp via the FF 24 SIB primitive
//     (PA-R15-009a / #1031). Both are listed as in-flight backtracks
//     against the v0.15 milestone.
//
// So at THIS landing, `@jump_table` is a parse-and-diagnose-only
// attribute: writing `match nic_kind @jump_table { ... }` today either
// silently emits nothing extra (if the elaborator gates the attribute
// on codegen readiness) or emits an unresolved-symbol against the
// jump-table synthesis intrinsic. Neither is a routing primitive we
// can depend on at boot.
//
// The compare chain is the safe substitute:
//
//   cmp rdi, 1;   je nd_tx_e1000e;
//   cmp rdi, 2;   je nd_tx_virtio;
//   cmp rdi, 3;   je nd_tx_rtl8139;
//   jmp nd_tx_bad;
//
// Three immediate compares, no rodata table, no memory-indirect jump.
// At three arms the compare chain is not measurably slower than the
// jump table would be even AFTER the codegen lands -- the jump table's
// advantage only appears once the arm count exceeds ~8 (see
// design/toolchain/command-dispatch-pattern.md's density contract:
// `covered*2 >= range` AND `range <= 256` -- three arms in a range of
// four is at the low end of what the jump table is worth). Adding a
// fourth NIC kind is still a one-line insertion into the cmp chain.
//
// FUTURE HOIST. When PA-R15-009b lands and the paideia-as smoke suite
// carries a witness that lowers a match with `@jump_table` to real
// FF 24 SIB + disp32, a future paideia-os issue may rewrite the four
// cmp chains here as annotated matches -- but the visible behaviour
// this file guarantees is unchanged.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1  WHY EVERY BACKEND ARM RETURNS -ENOSYS TODAY
// =============================================================================
//
// R27 landed e1000e's PCI probe, register accessors, ring init,
// EEPROM MAC read and IRQ handler shape -- but NOT a uniform
// (packet_ptr, packet_len) send entry point. Today's TX path is
// `l2_tx_send`, whose SysV signature is
// (bar0, tx_ring_pa, dst_mac, ethertype, payload_pa, payload_len).
// A future R91.M2-004 issue introduces an e1000e-side `e1000e_tx(
// packet_ptr, packet_len)` adapter that looks up `bar0` and
// `tx_ring_pa` from `_e1000e_devices[0]` and calls into the ring
// primitives directly; that adapter is the callee this shim's
// e1000e arm will call.
//
// Neither `virtio_net_probe` nor `virtio_net_tx` / `rx_poll` / `mac`
// exist in the kernel tree today -- R91.M3 introduces the from-
// scratch virtio-net driver under `src/kernel/core/drivers/virtio_net/`.
// The kernel-tree-external `src/drivers/virtio_net/probe.pdx` is a
// Phase-7-era skeleton retired by R91.M6-001 and is deliberately NOT
// wired here.
//
// The rtl8139 tree does not yet exist -- R91.M4 introduces it under
// `src/kernel/core/drivers/rtl8139/`.
//
// So at this landing, the tx / rx_poll / mac router arms all return
// the same sentinel -- `NIC_ENOSYS` = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA (=  -38 as
// signed u64, matching the ENOSYS convention `syscall/dispatch.pdx`
// uses for unwired sysno arms). This is the "future R91.M2-004 issues
// wire real bodies" posture the issue directive calls for. Each arm
// has its own labelled slot in the compare chain so that a future
// wiring is a one-line edit at exactly that slot, without touching
// the router topology or the sibling arms.
//
// `nic_dispatch_probe` today wires two backend-side probes: the
// e1000e arm (R27-M1-001, drivers/e1000e/probe.pdx) and — as of
// R91.M3-001 (paideia-os #2019) — the virtio-net arm (drivers/
// virtio_net/probe.pdx). Both distinguish "no NIC of that kind"
// from "at least one present, mint KIND_NIC with the matching
// nic_kind selector" for R91.M5-001's boot-order fan-out. The
// rtl8139 arm remains a labelled fall-through until R91.M4-001
// replaces the `jmp` at `nd_pb_try_rtl8139` with a real `call
// rtl8139_probe`.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2  SINGLE ACTIVE NIC PER BOOT
// =============================================================================
//
// design/networking/r91-plan.md §4.1 pins the MVP scope: one active
// NIC per boot, first-found wins, no multi-NIC bonding or selection.
// The single global `_active_nic_kind` (u64, .bss, NIC_KIND_NONE = 0
// at reset) is what this scope shrinks to. A future multi-NIC round
// promotes this to an array indexed by an `iface_id`, and every
// caller that reads it today reads through an accessor
// (`nic_dispatch_active_kind`) so the promotion is a signature change
// only in this file.
//
// `nic_dispatch_probe` is the SINGLE WRITER of `_active_nic_kind`
// (plus `nic_dispatch_reset` for teardown / bring-up-witness use).
// tools/build.sh confines relocations against the symbol to
// `net/nic_dispatch.o`, so no second object can silently mutate it.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3  ENCODER DISCIPLINE
// =============================================================================
//
// Kind selectors 1..3 fit trivially into a 32-bit immediate, so the
// compare chain uses `cmp reg, imm` directly. `NIC_ENOSYS` is a full
// 64-bit constant (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA sign-extends from -38 as u64)
// and stages through `mov rax, imm64` -- paideia-as's canonical form
// for a full-width immediate. No `mov_b` / `mov_w` / `mov_d`
// anywhere; every value lives in a whole 64-bit register. Every
// label in this file is prefixed `nd_` (nic-dispatch) per reserved-
// word discipline (`loop`/`if`/`next` are keywords).

module NicDispatch = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Kind selectors (mirror of cap/kind_nic.pdx §1). Re-declared here so
  // consumers of the dispatch shim do not need to cross-reference the
  // capability module for the enum -- the router's compare chain is the
  // one place these values are load-bearing at runtime.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let NIC_KIND_NONE    : u64 = 0
  pub let NIC_KIND_E1000E  : u64 = 1
  pub let NIC_KIND_VIRTIO  : u64 = 2
  pub let NIC_KIND_RTL8139 : u64 = 3
  pub let NIC_KIND_MAX     : u64 = 3

  // ==========================================================================
  // NIC_ENOSYS: the sentinel every unwired backend arm returns. Matches
  // `syscall/dispatch.pdx`'s ENOSYS convention (-38 as signed u64 =
  // 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA). Chosen so a caller that threads a nic_dispatch
  // return value straight into a syscall reply path (R91.M1-003+) sees
  // the same ENOSYS it would see from any other unwired sysno.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let NIC_ENOSYS : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA

  // ==========================================================================
  // _active_nic_kind: §2's single global. NIC_KIND_NONE at reset;
  // nic_dispatch_probe is the sole writer on the discover path,
  // nic_dispatch_reset is the sole writer on the teardown path.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _active_nic_kind : u64 = 0

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_probe_ok_msg: fingerprint emitted at the tail of every
  // nic_dispatch_probe call (R91.M2-001, paideia-os #2015). The line
  // carries the winning kind as `kind=<n>` via klog_s1_d1 (SUBSYS_NET_,
  // k_kind, rax) -- 0 on the no-device path, 1..3 once the corresponding
  // arm is wired. Two-shape body ("nic dispatch probe ok [legacy: NIC
  // DISPATCH PROBE OK]") mirrors the R51 Phase C bracketed-legacy
  // convention (see keys.pdx tag_mcfg_present, tag_e1000e_probe): the
  // English half is the primary marker, the bracketed all-caps legacy
  // suffix keeps operator grep parity with the pre-R51 wire. Byte count
  // 54 NUL-inclusive per #699 discipline. Declared here rather than in
  // klog/keys.pdx because it is the sole emit site for this tag today,
  // matching kind_nic.pdx's local nic_mint_ok_msg posture.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_probe_ok_msg : [u8; 54] = "nic dispatch probe ok [legacy: NIC DISPATCH PROBE OK]\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_reset() -> ()
  //
  // Clear _active_nic_kind. Boot and teardown only. .bss is already
  // zero at real boot; this is defensive (mirrors kn_rs_ /
  // hk_rs_reset shape) and gives a bring-up witness a clean re-scrub
  // point between two probe passes. Leaf. Labels prefixed nd_rs_.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012): clear _active_nic_kind. Boot and teardown only. Single-store leaf, no nested calls. .bss is already zero at real boot; this is defensive (mirrors kind_nic.pdx nic_table_reset / hotkey_dispatch.pdx hotkey_dispatch_reset shape) and gives a bring-up witness a clean re-scrub point between two probe passes. Labels prefixed nd_rs_.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rip + _active_nic_kind], rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_active_kind() -> u64
  //
  // Read the current active-NIC selector. The one accessor R91.M1-003
  // and R91.M1-004 call-site refactors thread through. Leaf. Labels
  // prefixed nd_ak_.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_active_kind : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012): read _active_nic_kind. §2's single accessor -- reading through the accessor rather than the raw symbol means a future multi-NIC promotion (index by iface_id) is a signature change here alone. Leaf, no nested calls. Labels prefixed nd_ak_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _active_nic_kind];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_probe() -> u64
  //
  // Walk the boot-order candidates in the order design/networking/
  // r91-plan.md §4.1 pins -- e1000e first, then virtio-net, then
  // rtl8139 -- calling each backend's own probe helper and stopping at
  // the first one that reports a device. On success writes the winning
  // NIC_KIND_* back to _active_nic_kind and returns it; on no-device
  // writes NIC_KIND_NONE and returns 0.
  //
  // At this landing the ONLY live backend probe is e1000e_probe (R27-
  // M1-001, drivers/e1000e/probe.pdx), which returns the count of
  // discovered e1000e / i219 NICs (0..E1000E_MAX_DEVS=2). Any non-zero
  // return means "an e1000e is here" -- the shim collapses that to
  // NIC_KIND_E1000E because §2's MVP scope carries one active NIC per
  // boot; the multi-device selection is R28+ work.
  //
  // The virtio-net and rtl8139 arms are labelled fall-throughs today.
  // R91.M3-001 replaces the `jmp nd_pb_try_rtl8139` at nd_pb_try_virtio
  // with a real `call virtio_net_probe`, and R91.M4-001 does the same
  // at nd_pb_try_rtl8139 for rtl8139_probe. Preserving the labels now
  // keeps those future edits a one-line insertion at the named slot,
  // without touching the topology.
  //
  // No callee-save registers needed -- across the single e1000e_probe
  // call we do not need any prior state, and the two fall-through arms
  // do not call anything today. Effects follow the underlying probe:
  // !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} matches e1000e_probe's declaration one-for-
  // one, which is why this helper is boot-only. Labels prefixed nd_pb_.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_probe : () -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012) + R91.M2-001 (paideia-os #2015) + R91.M3-001 (paideia-os #2019): boot-order NIC probe. Order pinned by design/networking/r91-plan.md §4.1 (e1000e -> virtio-net -> rtl8139; first-found wins). Calls e1000e_probe (drivers/e1000e/probe.pdx §R27-M1-001) first; any non-zero return collapses to NIC_KIND_E1000E under §2's single-active-NIC MVP scope. Falls through to virtio_net_probe (drivers/virtio_net/probe.pdx §R91.M3-001) on the no-e1000e path; any non-zero return collapses to NIC_KIND_VIRTIO. The rtl8139 arm remains a labelled fall-through today -- R91.M4-001 replaces `jmp nd_pb_none` at nd_pb_try_rtl8139 with `call rtl8139_probe`. No-device path writes NIC_KIND_NONE (0) and returns 0. Sole writer of _active_nic_kind on the discover path (nic_dispatch_reset is the sole writer on the teardown path). Effects !{sysreg, mem} @{boot} match e1000e_probe one-for-one. R91.M2-001 (paideia-os #2015) wiring: kernel_main_64 now calls nic_dispatch_probe once, immediately after pci_publish_caps (so _pci_devices is populated and the KIND_PCI_DEV caps that a driver-attach path will consume already exist). Every terminal arm converges on nd_pb_emit, which emits `nic dispatch probe ok [legacy: NIC DISPATCH PROBE OK] kind=<n>` via klog_s1_d1(LEVEL_INFO, SUBSYS_NET_, nic_dispatch_probe_ok_msg, k_kind, rax) BEFORE returning -- the fingerprint is the assertable signal in the default boot log that the probe actually ran, distinct from e1000e_probe's own \"E1000E PROBE N=<count>\" line which fires from inside the e1000e arm iff it was reached. Register/alignment plan: push r12 at prologue serves both as SysV callee-save preservation AND as rsp alignment (entry rsp%16==8 -> push r12 -> rsp%16==0, aligned per SysV for the nested `call e1000e_probe` and `call klog_s1_d1`); r12 holds the winning kind across the klog call so klog_s1_d1's caller-save clobber of rax does not eat the return value. Labels prefixed nd_pb_.",
      block: {
        // Prologue: push r12 serves both as SysV callee-save preservation
        // AND as rsp alignment (entry rsp%16==8 -> push r12 -> rsp%16==0,
        // aligned per SysV for both nested calls below). r12 will hold
        // the winning kind across klog_s1_d1 at nd_pb_emit so the caller-
        // save clobber of rax does not eat the return value.
        push r12;

        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        // Arm 1: e1000e (R27-M1-001, live).
        // ------------------------------------------------------------
        call e1000e_probe;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  nd_pb_try_virtio;
        mov rax, 1;                          // NIC_KIND_E1000E
        mov [rip + _active_nic_kind], rax;
        jmp nd_pb_emit;

      nd_pb_try_virtio:
        // R91.M3-001 (paideia-os #2019) wiring. virtio_net_probe
        // (drivers/virtio_net/probe.pdx) returns rax = count of matched
        // virtio-net devices in _pci_devices (0..VIRTIO_NET_MAX_DEVS=4);
        // any non-zero return collapses to NIC_KIND_VIRTIO under §2's
        // single-active-NIC MVP scope, mirroring the e1000e arm above.
        // The probe itself emits its own count fingerprint (SUBSYS_NET_,
        // tag_virtio_probe_ok, k_count) unconditionally; this arm's
        // subsequent nd_pb_emit fingerprint then carries kind=2 so the
        // pair is grep-assertable together in the boot log.
        call virtio_net_probe;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  nd_pb_try_rtl8139;
        // R91.M3-002 debugger-fix (paideia-os #2020): drive the virtio
        // 1.0 handshake now that _virtio_net_common_cfg_pa is populated
        // by virtio_net_probe. Handshake returns 0 on OK, negative on
        // fail; collapse to NIC_KIND_VIRTIO regardless (the fingerprint
        // pair `virtio-net probe ok` + `virtio-net init handshake ok`
        // is the grep-assertable OK signal; a FAIL leaves the OK line
        // absent).
        call virtio_net_init_handshake;
        mov rax, 2;                          // NIC_KIND_VIRTIO
        mov [rip + _active_nic_kind], rax;
        jmp nd_pb_emit;

      nd_pb_try_rtl8139:
        // R91.M4-001 replaces this fall-through with:
        //     call rtl8139_probe;
        //     cmp rax, 0;
        //     je  nd_pb_none;
        //     mov rax, 3;                   // NIC_KIND_RTL8139
        //     mov [rip + _active_nic_kind], rax;
        //     jmp nd_pb_emit;
        jmp nd_pb_none;

      nd_pb_none:
        xor rax, rax;                        // NIC_KIND_NONE
        mov [rip + _active_nic_kind], rax;

      nd_pb_emit:
        // R91.M2-001 (#2015) fingerprint: `nic dispatch probe ok
        // [legacy: NIC DISPATCH PROBE OK] kind=<n>` via klog_s1_d1
        // (LEVEL_INFO, SUBSYS_NET_, nic_dispatch_probe_ok_msg, k_kind,
        // rax). rax holds the winning kind (0..3); stash in callee-save
        // r12 across the klog call because klog_s1_d1 clobbers rax.
        mov r12, rax;                        // preserve kind
        mov rdi, 3;                          // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_NET_];
        lea rdx, [rip + nic_dispatch_probe_ok_msg];
        lea rcx, [rip + k_kind];
        mov r8, rax;                         // v1 = kind
        call klog_s1_d1;
        mov rax, r12;                        // restore kind -> rax
        pop r12;                             // restore caller's r12
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_tx(nic_kind, packet_ptr, packet_len) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = nic_kind (one of NIC_KIND_E1000E / VIRTIO / RTL8139)
  //   rsi = packet_ptr (physical address of a built L2 frame)
  //   rdx = packet_len (bytes)
  //
  // The compare chain fans out to per-backend TX adapters. At this
  // landing every arm returns NIC_ENOSYS -- see §1 for why (R27 landed
  // ring primitives but no uniform `e1000e_tx(pkt, len)` adapter;
  // virtio-net + rtl8139 drivers land in R91.M3 / R91.M4). Each arm
  // has its own labelled slot so a future wiring is a one-line edit
  // at exactly the slot without touching the router topology.
  //
  // An unknown / out-of-range nic_kind falls to nd_tx_bad and returns
  // NIC_ENOSYS as well -- caller receives the same sentinel it would
  // get from any unwired arm, which is the right thing at this shim's
  // altitude: distinguishing "unknown kind" from "unwired kind" is
  // work for a later observability landing, not for the router.
  //
  // Leaf across the compare chain (the wired arms will call adapters
  // that themselves push callee-saves; the router does not). Labels
  // prefixed nd_tx_.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_tx : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (nic_kind: u64, packet_ptr: u64, packet_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012): TX router. cmp/je chain over NIC_KIND_E1000E (1) / VIRTIO (2) / RTL8139 (3) with a no-match fall-through to NIC_ENOSYS. §1: every arm returns NIC_ENOSYS today because no backend `<x>_tx(packet_ptr, packet_len)` adapter exists in-tree yet (R27 landed e1000e's ring primitives via l2_tx_send's 6-arg surface, not a uniform 2-arg adapter; virtio-net + rtl8139 drivers land in R91.M3 / R91.M4). Each arm's slot is labelled so a future wiring (R91.M2-004 / R91.M3-004 / R91.M4-003) is a one-line edit -- replace `mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA; jmp nd_tx_out` at the target arm with `call <backend>_tx; jmp nd_tx_out`. Unknown nic_kind falls to nd_tx_bad and returns NIC_ENOSYS as well. Effects !{sysreg, mem} @{} -- no @{boot}, so the router is callable from steady-state paths (which is the whole point of the dispatch layer). Labels prefixed nd_tx_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 1;                          // NIC_KIND_E1000E
        je  nd_tx_e1000e;
        cmp rdi, 2;                          // NIC_KIND_VIRTIO
        je  nd_tx_virtio;
        cmp rdi, 3;                          // NIC_KIND_RTL8139
        je  nd_tx_rtl8139;
        jmp nd_tx_bad;

      nd_tx_e1000e:
        // R91.M2-004 wires: call e1000e_tx; jmp nd_tx_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;         // NIC_ENOSYS
        jmp nd_tx_out;

      nd_tx_virtio:
        // R91.M3-004 wires: call virtio_net_tx; jmp nd_tx_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_tx_out;

      nd_tx_rtl8139:
        // R91.M4-003 wires: call rtl8139_tx; jmp nd_tx_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_tx_out;

      nd_tx_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;

      nd_tx_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_rx_poll(nic_kind) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = nic_kind (one of NIC_KIND_E1000E / VIRTIO / RTL8139)
  //
  // Poll the active NIC's RX ring for a ready packet. Return value
  // shape mirrors the per-backend rx_poll adapter that will fill each
  // arm: a NIC_KIND_* sentinel on the "packet is here, handled" path,
  // NIC_KIND_NONE (0) on the "no packet" path, NIC_ENOSYS on the
  // unwired path. At this landing every arm is unwired -- see §1.
  //
  // Every arm has its own labelled slot so R91.M1-004's e1000e IRQ
  // handler refactor + the later R91.M3-005 / R91.M4-002 landings
  // each become a one-line replacement of `mov rax, ENOSYS; jmp
  // nd_rp_out` with `call <backend>_rx_poll; jmp nd_rp_out`. Labels
  // prefixed nd_rp_.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_rx_poll : (u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (nic_kind: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012): RX-poll router. cmp/je chain over the three NIC kinds with a no-match fall-through to NIC_ENOSYS. §1: every arm returns NIC_ENOSYS today (R91.M1-004 wires e1000e_rx_poll into nd_rp_e1000e; R91.M3-005 wires virtio_net_rx_poll into nd_rp_virtio; R91.M4-002 wires rtl8139_rx_poll into nd_rp_rtl8139). Return convention when arms land: NIC_KIND_* sentinel on the packet-handled path, NIC_KIND_NONE (0) on no-packet, NIC_ENOSYS on unwired. Effects !{sysreg, mem} @{} matches nd_tx one-for-one; no @{boot}, so the router is callable from ISR-adjacent paths (which is what R91.M1-004 needs). Labels prefixed nd_rp_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 1;                          // NIC_KIND_E1000E
        je  nd_rp_e1000e;
        cmp rdi, 2;                          // NIC_KIND_VIRTIO
        je  nd_rp_virtio;
        cmp rdi, 3;                          // NIC_KIND_RTL8139
        je  nd_rp_rtl8139;
        jmp nd_rp_bad;

      nd_rp_e1000e:
        // R91.M1-004 diverged: rather than wiring e1000e_rx_poll here,
        // it introduced the sibling `nic_dispatch_rx_dispatch` shim
        // (below) which is what e1000e_irq_handler now calls per
        // descriptor. This poll arm stays unwired until a future
        // issue introduces a polling caller (e.g. an idle-loop RX
        // drain independent of MSI-X delivery).
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;         // NIC_ENOSYS
        jmp nd_rp_out;

      nd_rp_virtio:
        // R91.M3-005 wires: call virtio_net_rx_poll; jmp nd_rp_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_rp_out;

      nd_rp_rtl8139:
        // R91.M4-002 wires: call rtl8139_rx_poll; jmp nd_rp_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_rp_out;

      nd_rp_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;

      nd_rp_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_rx_dispatch(nic_kind, packet_ptr, packet_len) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = nic_kind (one of NIC_KIND_E1000E / VIRTIO / RTL8139)
  //   rsi = packet_ptr (physical address of a received L2 frame,
  //                     identity-mapped under KERNEL_VMA_BASE)
  //   rdx = packet_len (bytes; matches the RX descriptor's length
  //                     field, which already excludes the HW-stripped
  //                     4-byte FCS)
  //
  // The generic entry point every driver's IRQ handler calls when it
  // has drained a completed RX descriptor and needs to hand the frame
  // body up to the L2 receive dispatcher (l2_rx_handle in
  // core/net/l2_rx.pdx). The e1000e arm is a pass-through: it re-
  // shuffles (packet_ptr, packet_len) from (rsi, rdx) into
  // l2_rx_handle's (rdi, rsi) SysV slots and calls, propagating
  // l2_rx_handle's rax (0 accepted / 1 parse-or-length failure) back
  // to the caller unchanged.
  //
  // WHY A SHIM RATHER THAN AN INLINE `call l2_rx_handle` PER DRIVER.
  // R91.M3 introduces virtio-net and R91.M4 introduces rtl8139; both
  // will export their own ISR body that drains their own RX ring. If
  // each ISR called l2_rx_handle directly, l2_rx.pdx would grow N
  // reverse-edges (one per driver) that any change to l2_rx_handle's
  // signature or side-effect posture (adding a caller-provided
  // subsys tag, moving the RX-pkts counter into a per-NIC slot, ...)
  // would have to update at N call sites -- exactly the coupling
  // R91's dispatch plan is meant to break. Routing through this shim
  // keeps l2_rx_handle's caller-set at ONE (this file), so a future
  // signature change is a one-line update here plus the shim's arm.
  //
  // The virtio-net and rtl8139 arms return NIC_ENOSYS today because
  // no such driver exists in-tree yet -- see §1. When R91.M3 lands,
  // nd_rd_virtio's `mov rax, NIC_ENOSYS; jmp nd_rd_out` becomes
  // `jmp nd_rd_do_l2rx` (the pass-through shape is NIC-agnostic
  // -- every driver whose ISR calls this router hands off the frame
  // body to l2_rx_handle by the same (packet_ptr, packet_len)
  // convention); R91.M4 does the same at nd_rd_rtl8139.
  //
  // Return convention: mirrors l2_rx_handle for the wired arm
  //   (0 = accepted-and-dispatched, 1 = parse / length failure),
  // NIC_ENOSYS on unwired virtio / rtl8139 arms today, NIC_ENOSYS
  // on unknown / out-of-range nic_kind at nd_rd_bad.
  //
  // Effects !{sysreg, mem} @{} mirror nd_tx / nd_rp -- no @{boot},
  // so the router is callable from ISR-adjacent paths (which is the
  // whole point of this shim). This is the ONE nic_dispatch entry
  // that IS on an ISR path today, via e1000e_irq_handler's per-
  // descriptor call. Labels prefixed nd_rd_.
  //
  // Register plan: no callee-save pushes needed. Entry rsp%16==8;
  // after `call l2_rx_handle`'s implicit push rsp%16==0 -- aligned
  // for l2_rx_handle's own prologue without any pad here. No local
  // scratch, no globals touched -- just a cmp/je fan-out to a
  // register-shuffle plus a nested call in the e1000e arm.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_rx_dispatch : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (nic_kind: u64, packet_ptr: u64, packet_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R91.M1-004 (paideia-os #2014): generic RX-dispatch shim. Every driver's IRQ handler calls this instead of poking l2_rx_handle directly, which keeps l2_rx_handle's caller-set at 1 (this file) as virtio-net (R91.M3) and rtl8139 (R91.M4) join e1000e. Body: cmp/je chain over the three NIC kinds with a no-match fall-through to NIC_ENOSYS. §1: virtio + rtl8139 arms return NIC_ENOSYS today because no such driver exists in-tree yet; when R91.M3-00X / R91.M4-00X land, each arm's `mov rax, NIC_ENOSYS; jmp nd_rd_out` becomes `jmp nd_rd_do_l2rx` (the pass-through into l2_rx_handle is NIC-agnostic -- driver ISRs adopt the same (packet_ptr, packet_len) handoff convention as the e1000e arm). The e1000e arm re-shuffles (packet_ptr, packet_len) from (rsi, rdx) into l2_rx_handle's (rdi, rsi) SysV slots and calls, propagating l2_rx_handle's rax (0 accepted / 1 parse-or-length failure) back unchanged. Unknown nic_kind falls to nd_rd_bad and returns NIC_ENOSYS as well. Effects !{sysreg, mem} @{} match nd_tx / nd_rp / nd_mc; this is the ONE nic_dispatch entry that IS on an ISR path (via e1000e_irq_handler's per-descriptor call). No callee-save pushes: entry rsp%16==8; after the nested `call l2_rx_handle`'s implicit push rsp%16==0 -- aligned for l2_rx_handle's own prologue without any pad here. l2_rx_handle preserves rbx/rbp/r12/r13 per SysV, so the caller's callee-save state survives across this shim unchanged. Labels prefixed nd_rd_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 1;                          // NIC_KIND_E1000E
        je  nd_rd_e1000e;
        cmp rdi, 2;                          // NIC_KIND_VIRTIO
        je  nd_rd_virtio;
        cmp rdi, 3;                          // NIC_KIND_RTL8139
        je  nd_rd_rtl8139;
        jmp nd_rd_bad;

      nd_rd_e1000e:
        // Pass-through: hand (packet_ptr, packet_len) to l2_rx_handle.
        // R91.M3-00X / R91.M4-00X will point their arms at
        // nd_rd_do_l2rx (a labelled entry inside this pass-through)
        // once their drivers land; today the e1000e arm is the only
        // NIC on the path, so an inline shuffle here is enough.
      nd_rd_do_l2rx:
        mov rdi, rsi;                        // rdi = packet_ptr
        mov rsi, rdx;                        // rsi = packet_len
        call l2_rx_handle;
        jmp nd_rd_out;

      nd_rd_virtio:
        // R91.M3-00X wires: jmp nd_rd_do_l2rx.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;         // NIC_ENOSYS
        jmp nd_rd_out;

      nd_rd_rtl8139:
        // R91.M4-00X wires: jmp nd_rd_do_l2rx.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_rd_out;

      nd_rd_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;

      nd_rd_out:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // nic_dispatch_mac(nic_kind, out_mac_ptr) -> u64
  //
  //   rdi = nic_kind (one of NIC_KIND_E1000E / VIRTIO / RTL8139)
  //   rsi = out_mac_ptr (caller-provided 6-byte buffer)
  //
  // Copy the active NIC's 6-byte MAC into the caller's buffer.
  // Return 0 on success, NIC_ENOSYS on the unwired arm. At this
  // landing every arm is unwired -- see §1.
  //
  // NOTE ON E1000E. `drivers/e1000e/mac.pdx` exports e1000e_read_mac
  // (bar0, out_mac_ptr) -> () which DOES read MAC bytes from
  // EEPROM/OTP words 0/1/2 into a caller buffer. Wiring nd_mac_e1000e
  // to it today would require this shim to look up `bar0` from
  // `_e1000e_devices[0]+8` -- a cross-module read against a table
  // that is only populated once e1000e_probe has run. That coupling
  // (implicit dependency on probe having completed, no error for the
  // pre-probe caller) is exactly the kind of surface a future
  // R91.M2-004 adapter -- `e1000e_mac(out_mac_ptr)` that looks up
  // bar0 internally AND returns an errno on unprobed-device -- is
  // meant to hide from this shim. Deferring to that adapter keeps
  // this router's arms uniform (all return either 0 or NIC_ENOSYS,
  // never an implicit-dependency-tripped SIGSEGV).
  //
  // Labels prefixed nd_mc_.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let nic_dispatch_mac : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{sysreg, mem} @{} =
    fn (nic_kind: u64, out_mac_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg, mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R91.M1-002 (paideia-os #2012): MAC-read router. cmp/je chain over the three NIC kinds with a no-match fall-through to NIC_ENOSYS. §1: every arm returns NIC_ENOSYS today. e1000e_read_mac (drivers/e1000e/mac.pdx §R27-M1-004) exists but takes (bar0, out_mac) -- wiring it into nd_mc_e1000e would couple the shim to _e1000e_devices[0]+8 as an implicit read of state populated only after e1000e_probe, and a pre-probe caller would trip a SIGSEGV against unmapped MMIO. R91.M2-004 introduces a bar0-hiding adapter (e1000e_mac(out_mac_ptr) that returns an errno on unprobed-device); this router's e1000e arm calls THAT adapter, keeping arms uniform (each returns 0 on success or NIC_ENOSYS on the unwired / unprobed path). Return convention: 0 on success, NIC_ENOSYS on unwired. Effects !{sysreg, mem} @{} matches nd_tx / nd_rp. Labels prefixed nd_mc_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 1;                          // NIC_KIND_E1000E
        je  nd_mc_e1000e;
        cmp rdi, 2;                          // NIC_KIND_VIRTIO
        je  nd_mc_virtio;
        cmp rdi, 3;                          // NIC_KIND_RTL8139
        je  nd_mc_rtl8139;
        jmp nd_mc_bad;

      nd_mc_e1000e:
        // R91.M2-004 wires: mov rdi, rsi; call e1000e_mac; jmp nd_mc_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;         // NIC_ENOSYS
        jmp nd_mc_out;

      nd_mc_virtio:
        // R91.M3-006 wires: mov rdi, rsi; call virtio_net_mac; jmp nd_mc_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_mc_out;

      nd_mc_rtl8139:
        // R91.M4-004 wires: mov rdi, rsi; call rtl8139_mac; jmp nd_mc_out.
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;
        jmp nd_mc_out;

      nd_mc_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA;

      nd_mc_out:
        ret
      }
    }
}
